{"id":"290b1d0a-75ce-4019-86bb-014fb09a08b8","question":"Can you explain the difference between LSTM and GRU?","timestamp":"2026-01-14T15:01:55.836977","model_a":"kicampus-v1","answer_a":"LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory) and GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) are both types of recurrent neural networks designed to handle sequence data. LSTM has three gates (input, output, and forget) which control the flow of information, while GRU has two gates (reset and update) and is generally simpler and faster to compute. Both are used to solve the vanishing gradient problem in RNNs.<a href=\"https://ki-campus.org/node/673\"><sup>[1]</sup></a>","model_b":"kicampus-v1-improved","answer_b":"LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory) and GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit) are both types of recurrent neural networks designed to handle sequence data. LSTM has three gates (input, output, and forget) to control the flow of information, while GRU has two gates (reset and update) and is generally simpler and faster to train. Both are used to solve the vanishing gradient problem in RNNs.<a href=\"https://ki-campus.org/node/673\"><sup>[1]</sup></a>","vote":"B","vote_timestamp":"2026-01-14T15:21:44.912999","comment":null,"subset_id":1}
